  {"text": "This chapter explains how the basic timer counts, reloads and signals events. The counter is sixteen bits wide and is clocked through a programmable prescaler, so a single timer can measure intervals from under a microsecond to several seconds. Firmware usually starts by selecting the clock source, then writes the prescaler (PSC) and the auto-reload value (ARR), and finally sets the CEN bit in the control register to start counting. Whenever the counter wraps around, the hardware raises an update flag; if the matching interrupt is enabled, the CPU jumps to the handler, which must clear the flag before returning, otherwise the interrupt fires again immediately. Writing a new reload value while the timer is running is safe only when the preload buffer is enabled, because the new value is then copied at the next update instead of taking effect in the middle of a period. Several timers can be chained: one timer's update output may drive another timer's clock input, which builds a 32-bit counter out of two 16-bit ones. In low-power designs, remember that the timer clock stops in Stop mode, so elapsed time must be recovered from the real-time clock after wake-up. Typical values are listed in Table 42: with a 48 MHz kernel clock and PSC = 47, one tick lasts exactly 1 µs, and ARR = 999 gives a 1 kHz update rate (±0.5% depending on the oscillator trim). Common mistakes include forgetting to enable the peripheral clock in the RCC register, reading the counter before the first update event, and assuming that the flag is cleared automatically. When debugging, halt the core and inspect TIMx_CNT, TIMx_SR and TIMx_DIER; a counter stuck at zero almost always means the clock is gated. For example, the sequence RCC->APB1ENR |= RCC_APB1ENR_TIM2EN; TIM2->PSC = 47; TIM2->ARR = 999; TIM2->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN; starts a 1 ms tick, e.g., for a scheduler. See also the errata sheet, section 2.4.1, for a known issue with the update flag. Values in parentheses are typical; minimum and maximum limits apply over the full temperature range.", "ids": [84, 4868, 4356, 135, 5, 1837, 82, 235, 14316, 4, 29182, 6, 9, 5559, 1490, 3, 21, 4993, 12, 16558, 12234, 1408, 9, 12, 4577, 64, 200, 10, 24969, 1309, 5965, 235, 4, 88, 10, 627, 82, 235, 38, 2720, 10950, 6, 37, 342, 10, 3821, 12034, 7, 787, 2358, 3, 27757, 1115, 3037, 45, 11765, 5, 4577, 1280, 4, 156, 8242, 5, 1309, 5965, 235, 29, 6145, 230, 40, 9, 5, 3840, 19, 98, 7028, 746, 29, 4362, 626, 486, 9, 1569, 4088, 5, 270, 4486, 1072, 11, 5, 389, 4652, 7, 451, 12189, 3, 10089, 5, 4993, 13428, 6, 307, 4, 5, 3357, 16522, 53, 2016, 6442, 75, 108, 5, 13207, 16396, 12, 5525, 4, 5, 7052, 5523, 6, 7, 5, 23140, 4, 94, 233, 853, 5, 6442, 185, 7652, 4, 3227, 5, 16396, 881, 6, 284, 1451, 3, 9982, 10, 125, 29182, 746, 331, 5, 82, 235, 12, 1508, 12, 704, 113, 99, 5, 1309, 7028, 13975, 12, 5525, 4, 194, 5, 125, 746, 12, 156, 17399, 46, 5, 356, 2016, 1382, 8, 1027, 1277, 11, 5, 2228, 8, 10, 918, 3, 7352, 82, 1205, 38, 26, 3855, 64, 20, 56, 82, 235, 18, 6, 2016, 2359, 118, 465, 276, 82, 235, 18, 6, 4577, 3737, 4, 94, 15171, 10, 14424, 2291, 4993, 97, 8, 157, 409, 19, 2291, 1384, 3, 77, 844, 19, 7713, 5785, 4, 767, 16, 5, 82, 235, 4577, 8574, 11, 3573, 1926, 4, 88, 824, 20838, 78, 82, 233, 26, 5393, 37, 5, 447, 19, 1030, 4577, 183, 4233, 19, 745, 3, 18524, 2379, 25, 3688, 11, 4165, 2577, 20, 22, 10, 2323, 16065, 11264, 4577, 9, 5579, 230, 2186, 4250, 4, 56, 12700, 314, 6, 1691, 71, 15, 0, 6, 4, 9, 7305, 626, 2186, 15, 11956, 1261, 10, 71, 15, 18976, 2016, 1086, 29, 10769, 10812, 1806, 4031, 28, 5, 3139, 6, 2124, 182, 3149, 16170, 316, 6225, 4775, 692, 17747, 7, 2970, 5, 21778, 4577, 11, 5, 456, 3913, 4652, 4, 1446, 5, 4993, 185, 5, 122, 2016, 1078, 4, 9, 19577, 16, 5, 6442, 12, 16557, 2338, 3, 186, 27426, 4, 14612, 5, 2672, 9, 14690, 373, 6751, 330, 594, 230, 8223, 4, 373, 6751, 330, 594, 11797, 9, 373, 6751, 330, 594, 6645, 2296, 75, 10, 4993, 6978, 46, 4955, 851, 261, 457, 5, 4577, 12, 4622, 78, 3, 144, 377, 4, 5, 6492, 4